 "result_text": "I fixed the precision loss in `DateTime.__add__`, which the issue's `testAddPrecision` failure comes from. The existing suite passes (48 passed). I haven't run it with the clock set past 2038-01-10, so I haven't confirmed the three listed failures are gone.\n\n`__add__` computed the exact integer microsecond count (`tmicros`). It then passed only a float seconds value to the constructor, and the constructor rebuilt the microseconds as `floor(t * 1e6)`. A float seconds value for a date in 2038 or later can't represent every microsecond, so the rebuild was off by a few microseconds. That matches the `\u2026959838` vs `\u2026959840` difference in the report.\n\nThe fix is one line, at `src/DateTime/DateTime.py:1763`. It passes `tmicros` to the constructor instead of `None`, so the exact value is kept.\n\nI didn't look into `testConstructor4` or `testSubtraction`. I'm assuming they fail for the same reason, since both are likely to go through `__add__`.\n\nI didn't touch any tests or commit anything.",
